{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "number": 45,
  "title": "Prop 45 \u2014 Faster project review: what would change and what is at stake?",
  "fps": 30,
  "frames": 6080,
  "checked": "2026-10-19",
  "narration": "asset://af363c56a9f336429d6fcd0b12641e6f99ce6fbc1887aa8a8a57cbea9eb63e35.wav",
  "prompter_words": "words-global.json",
  "script": [
    {
      "name": "The big picture",
      "narration": "Proposition forty-five changes environmental review for eligible construction projects. California\u2019s environmental quality law requires agencies to examine significant impacts before approving many projects. Review can identify harms and alternatives, while delay can also make projects more costly. The proposal offers an optional faster process for specified projects, including many housing, transportation, water, health, and clean-energy projects. It changes deadlines and the scope of review and litigation, not just the speed of paperwork."
    },
    {
      "name": "An illustrative example",
      "narration": "Imagine a planner preparing a new housing project. Residents may want homes built sooner but also want traffic, water, and environmental impacts examined. Those goals can overlap. The disagreement is whether the proposed process keeps enough scrutiny while reducing avoidable delay. The surrounding community lives with the completed project."
    },
    {
      "name": "How it would work",
      "narration": "For projects using the new route, agencies would set application requirements upfront and face tighter deadlines. For example, missing a three-hundred-sixty-five-business-day environmental-report deadline could trigger a hearing request and potentially a lawsuit. It is not automatic approval after a year. Review would use standards in place when the application is submitted. The applicant could offer one alternative instead of an agency developing several. Public-comment periods would have maximums, and required tribal consultation would be narrower. Courts would have less scope to review challenges and could stop the noncompliant portion rather than the whole project. Other permits and legal requirements still matter. Faster review does not mean every project becomes buildable or affordable. Initial government costs could reach the high tens of millions annually, potentially over one hundred million, partly covered by fees. Longer-term effects are uncertain: faster construction may save money, while additional environmental harm could create costs."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote yes",
      "narration": "A voter might support more predictable schedules and fewer project-wide shutdowns, hoping to deliver homes and infrastructure sooner. They may judge delay itself costly. That benefit depends on actual projects moving forward, not merely shorter review documents."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote no",
      "narration": "You might ask what evidence or voices could be missed. Another voter could object to fewer alternatives, tighter public participation, or narrower court and tribal review. They may worry that faster decisions shift costs onto communities or the environment. The question is the balance of speed and accountability, not whether review should exist at all."
    },
    {
      "name": "What polling indicates",
      "narration": "In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 49 percent said yes, 46 percent no, and 5 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not votes already cast."
    },
    {
      "name": "Make your own decision",
      "narration": "Yes creates the optional new procedures; no keeps existing review and challenge procedures. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice."
    }
  ],
